  "markdown": "## Business Model\nCostco operates membership warehouses and e-commerce sites globally, offering low prices on a limited selection of nationally-branded and private-label products [10-K line 00134]. The company uses a no-frills, self-service warehouse format with strict inventory controls to minimize shrinkage and reduce labor costs [10-K line 00139]. Key business components include core merchandise categories (foods and sundries, non-foods, fresh foods), warehouse ancillary services (gasoline, pharmacy, food court), and other businesses such as e-commerce, business centers, and travel [10-K line 00143]. Membership fees are a significant revenue source, with annual fees of $65 in the U.S., varying in other countries, including a free household card [10-K line 00157].\n\n## Competitive Position\nCostco competes globally with supermarkets, supercenters, online retailers, and other warehouse clubs like Sam’s Club and BJ’s Wholesale Club [10-K line 00190]. The company's competitive advantages include its membership format, which reinforces member loyalty and provides steady fee revenue; a focus on low prices and high-quality merchandise; and strong operational efficiency through volume purchasing and efficient distribution [10-K line 00134]. Costco differentiates itself through private-label products like Kirkland Signature, which are often priced lower than national brands and contribute to higher margins [10-K line 00192].\n\n## Growth Drivers\nCostco's growth is driven by increasing net sales, particularly comparable sales, which include new warehouse openings and member retention [10-K line 00398]. Membership fee revenue has grown due to new sign-ups, fee increases, and upgrades to Executive memberships [10-K line 00456]. Digitally-enabled sales represented 11% of total net sales in 2026, indicating a strong push towards online commerce [10-K line 00153]. Additionally, expansion into international markets with higher growth potential and improved operational efficiency support long-term growth [10-K line 00408].\n\n## Catalysts\nRecent catalysts include a 13% increase in the quarterly cash dividend approved by the Board of Directors in April 2026 [10-K line 00423]. The company also experienced positive impacts from higher gasoline prices, which increased net sales by $3,501 million or 130 basis points in 2026 [10-K line 00454]. Furthermore, IEEPA tariff refunds totaling $184 million were recognized during the year, with a portion invested in price reductions for members [10-K line 00426].\n\n## Recent Developments\nIn fiscal 2026, Costco opened 28 new warehouses (including three relocations), resulting in 25 net new warehouses [10-K line 00415]. Net sales increased 10% to $297.2 billion, with comparable sales growth of 8% [10-K line 00451]. Membership fees rose 11% to $5.907 billion [10-K line 00456]. The company reported a net income increase of 14% to $9.226 billion [10-K line 00422]. Additionally, Costco has been investing in capital expenditures, spending $6,435 million in 2026, with plans to spend approximately $7,500 million in fiscal 2027 [10-K line 00513].\n\n## Material Risks\nCostco faces risks related to economic conditions, including inflation or deflation affecting consumer spending patterns and merchandise costs [10-K line 00398]. Currency fluctuations impact international operations, particularly in the Other International and Canadian segments [10-K line 00409]. Competition remains intense across global, national, and regional retailers, with e-commerce operations posing significant competitive challenges [10-K line 00190]. Rising costs associated with employees, including health-care costs and wages, also pose a risk [10-K line 00407]. Tariff impacts have affected some merchandise costs, though the company’s exposure is dependent on the type of goods and timing of tariffs [10-K line 00403].",
